WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:02.760
Film Courage: What makes a great horror movie?

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.550
David F. Sandberg, Filmmaker and PonySmasher
on Youtube: It kind of depends on what kind

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:09.980
of movie because the horror movies I’ve
made have kind of been popcorn where it’s

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:19.850
kind of dry which is very satisfying because
you get this cheering when the right person

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:25.070
goes or gets away and people are like laughing
and then jumping and people are laughing about

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.330
jumping.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:40.260
So in those movies like that it’s very much
about the scares and

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:52.010
sound is super important and timing because
of lot of people don’t take their time for

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:57.030
the creepiness and the scares because you
can make anyone jump if you just have a loud

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.329
sudden noise.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:05.300
But it’s that Hitchcock thing with the thing
under the table and if something happens people

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.010
are going to go “Oh shout.”

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:12.380
But if they know that it’s something that
is going to happen and you are sitting there

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:13.420
at the end of your seat.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:19.509
I think that is something that will work very
well on LIGHTS OUT the short film is that

00:01:19.509 --> 00:01:25.640
we start right off with a big scare (the thing
just comes closer).

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:35.049
Now we’ve scared people and now people know
what to expect and then that makes her hiding

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:39.170
under the bed and hearing little things that
that makes it much more effective because

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.180
then you set the precedent.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:49.479
But then there are horror movies that again
that are more art house like HEREDITARY and

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:54.770
BLAIR WITCH that are about scares and really
like staying with you.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:02:05.520
And again I really think that sound is very
important.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.220
I mean David Lynch…I mean he’s not a horror
director but he’s made some of the scariest

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:23.780
stuff out there.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:30.099
What I try to do is make it as bad for the
characters as possible with this continuous

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:36.730
sort of escalation of it getting worse and
worse and worse.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:40.540
She runs into a bar and then she gets trapped
in it and “Oh no!”

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.209
And then there is a scarecrow and it starts
to come alive and it’s like “Oh no, this

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:45.209
is even worse!”

00:02:45.209 --> 00:02:55.709
Like a continuous escalation of just making
it worse and worse for you.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:03.379
Film Courage: You are used to using a lot
of things for the DIY when you were making

00:03:03.379 --> 00:03:04.760
short films back in Sweden.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:10.110
What were some of those things you would use
for sound effects that you would use that

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.110
were inexpensive?

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:15.030
David: In our apartment we were blessed with
very creaky floors which was great.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:21.860
I have a whole library of me recorded of just
creaking around.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:33.319
And yes, just trying to find sounds like recording
the air ventilation system like slowing that

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:37.200
down and you have this sort of dark drone.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:46.650
That’s really effective.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:53.959
Lights and sounds and this bucket light from
an Ikea trash can or whatever we could do.

00:03:53.959 --> 00:04:00.360
I mean a lot of it would be based on things
we’ve seen of proper movies of like “Oohh…how

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.120
can I make a low-budget version of that?”

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.939
Which still sort of stays in my head.

00:04:05.939 --> 00:04:11.390
Because I remember on ANNABELLE: CREATION
they have these quasar lights which are basically

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:17.070
like tubes or whatever that are kind of expensive.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:23.229
But just watching it I was like I could use
that if I used a regular light tube and just

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.620
put it in the same way and put it in the same
way.

